docs/architecture.md:29:The active frame checks progression in the interface phase, after existing-avatar controls/HUD and before field combat. Ordinary upgrades/downgrades therefore observe the previous field frame's final health. Level-zero damage starts its escape immediately. Primary input and passive weapons use separate category passes through `tickGuns`; passive shots are born after the field's bullet loop. Primary triggers run before field combat; primary cooldowns/aim and passive firing run in the later avatar phase. HUD readouts are captured in the interface phase, so later health/counter changes cannot leak into the same displayed frame. Shared secondary readiness retains its distinct zero reset frame and advances during transit. Dialog return modes retain an in-progress crossing through upgrade choice and secondary help. Boundary detection precedes interface work; the initiating state0 frame still runs the avatar, while subsequent state2 frames run both fields without player status/passive updates. Arrival clears movement and the player shield, discards all registered effects/callbacks, and resets primary counters. The fidelity ledger identifies remaining source quirks, weapon callbacks and independent animation-clock work.
docs/architecture.md:31:`simulation/field-runtime.ts` owns each attached field's shots, pickups, allies and pending effects, plus collection/firing sound counters. `World` retains living enemies in its field cache, discards outgoing runtime collections on arrival, and preserves the destination's already-running shots, pickups and allies. Registered visual effects/callbacks from both fields are cleared by the source global-effects lifecycle on arrival. Both fields run during transit; new bullet insertion is rejected, but existing shots and weapon clocks continue. A synchronous field context projects the actual avatar into the destination's local coordinates and restores it with `try/finally`, so damage callbacks keep avatar identity. Rendering separately adds each field's offset to its local records and retains original tank identities for animated masks. Dead enemies leave at the next field frame, and the live bullet loop processes newly appended fragments in the same frame.
docs/architecture.md:39:Non-player actors advance movement/sticky, jam, ordered parts, protection/effects, then ink/infection/weaken and boundary checks. A stunned entry skips movement, parts and boundary for its complete reset frame while keeping protection/effects active. Enemy and ally arrays run independently without snapshots, allowing newly spawned enemies to run immediately. Player interface/passive ordering remains separate and its remaining source differences are tracked in the fidelity ledger.
docs/architecture.md:91:`tools/reference-player.ts` serves a separate loopback-only original SWF viewer with development dependency Ruffle 0.7.1. It shares the SWF decompressor, serves WASM with the correct MIME type, and accepts a browser-local file or explicit `BT3_SWF_FILE`. `reference-browser.ts` owns a headless Chrome/CDP instance with the stack size this movie needs; `reference-input.ts` uses Node Playwright for native coordinate input and stage captures. Ruffle is never imported by `src` or included in the Next.js output. The optional original-runtime smoke test is enabled with `BT3_REFERENCE=1`; it complements simulation tests rather than establishing whole-game parity.
docs/architecture.md:147:`simulation/whip.ts`, `grow.ts` and `flak.ts` separate active Run state from retained stage playback. Whip samples the named primary child's registration points at its previous location before following the emitter, retaining launch rotation. Grow changes explicit scale/alpha only during active movement and removes itself immediately on the next Run after becoming transparent. Flak polls its current root frame for its blast/removal, so paused playback can skip the blast entirely. Sapper holds root frames while child clips continue. The shared display-list builder has no browser dependency; generated metadata contains no raster, vector paths, fonts or audio. Exact original event-loop/constructor interleaving remains a runtime comparison boundary.
docs/architecture.md:252:First-use help reads the Boolean profile flag after loading. Only the last Next action writes completion; Escape closes without marking it seen. Supplied artwork and numerical mock regions retain the local guide while excluding the retired server callout. Replacement copy states the offline behavior. These local contracts do not establish original-runtime typography, paint, event-phase or full-game parity; remaining source differences stay in the fidelity ledger.
docs/architecture.md:257:`core/viewer-navigation.ts` keeps the source's persistent page/detail button flags in an immutable snapshot. `TankViewer` dispatches local, sort, code, cards, detail and page actions, then asks the existing pane owner to rebuild or transfer previews. Caption setters and handlers write flags in source order, so enabled controls cannot be derived solely from queue bounds. Repeated First/Next actions can deliberately rebuild the same page/tank. The model has no rendering, profile or browser-service dependency.
docs/architecture.md:303:`simulation/actor-movement.ts` now owns active enemy/ally translation and delayed turn plans; `Tank.movement` replaces legacy steering counters. Registered behavior parts run after primary weapons and movement through `behaviors.ts`, consuming the separate destination, target-acquisition and Phasing modules. `ActorParents` owns simulation-only parent records: it distinguishes actor removal from unrooting an outgoing field wrapper and reparenting cached actors. It supplies parent-relative points without renderer objects or an active-only UID lookup. Parent records currently derive from World camera snapshots; per-assignment camera precision and matrix edge cases remain fidelity work. Healer retains its existing beam/patient owner until its overridden acquisition/Aim rules are recovered.
docs/architecture.md:306:`simulation/weapon-targets.ts` uses the same direct-reference target state as behaviors, separately for each offensive mount. Setup acquisition occurs during registered-part construction. The World parent tracker supplies attachment and cross-field projection; per-part Run/Aim acquisition and post-Aim ally-count refresh precede firing. `simulation/healer-target.ts` owns the distinct source Healer selector. Healing beams retain their own patient object. A known missing-ally read aborts the current World listener through a scoped error type while retained art and subsequent stage frames continue; unrelated JavaScript errors are not suppressed. The tank-wide target selector and field-wide target resets are removed.
docs/architecture.md:309:`core/weapon-aim.ts` records effective source manual/enemy aim flags at mount Setup. B1's first-primary gate can then disable the registered enemy primary rotation flags during movement. Rotating machine-gun and orbital Run overrides bypass the generic target/Aim prefix. Non-Healer behaviors invoke their inherited Aim between Run acquisition and post-Aim ally-count refresh, so Phasing retains its inherited rotation. `simulation/spikes.ts` owns seven-emitter geometric contact, reference-based contact memory and ordered force writes independently of firing timers. Known unparented/deactivated Spike reads join the scoped World listener boundary; arbitrary host errors still escape. Area Burst removes queried hostile destroyable shots immediately through the central removal path.
